portunity costs, leaving us vulnerable and behind, as seen in the slower pace of development in the early days of biotechnology regulation compared to more adaptive sectors.\n\nInstead, genuine safety-critical engineering is best incentivized through adaptive, post-deployment mechanisms like empirical telemetry integrated with strict liability at the inference layer. This approach allows for rapid iteration, real-world learning, and targeted interventions, ensuring accountability where algorithmic harm materializes without stifling initial deployment. The public benefits from faster access to innovative, safer AI systems, while downstream deployers bear primary liability for harmful inferences, incentivizing them to demand robust, auditable systems from developers. Enforcement could leverage Section 5 of the FTC Act against unfair or deceptive practices, combined with new legislation establishing clear liability for harms traceable to specific AI inferences, compelling transparency and continuous improvement. The aviation industry's safety culture, relying on post-incident analysis and continuous data feedback through programs like the FAA's Aviation Safety Action Program, provides a powerful historical precedent for this adaptive, empirical safety paradigm.",
